([SOLVED PROBLEM 1: Comprehensive Calculation of Water Hardness from Mineral Analysis]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: A water sample from an industrial biotechnology supply contains the following) Tj
T*
(dissolved mineral salts:) Tj
T*
(Ca\(HCO3\)2 = 16.2 mg/L, Mg\(HCO3\)2 = 14.6 mg/L, CaSO4 = 13.6 mg/L,) Tj
T*
(MgCl2 = 9.5 mg/L, MgSO4 = 12.0 mg/L, NaCl = 58.5 mg/L, SiO2 = 15.0 mg/L.) Tj
T*
(Calculate: \(1\) Temporary Hardness in ppm, \(2\) Permanent Hardness in ppm, \(3\) Total) Tj
T*
(Hardness in ppm and French degrees.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Multiplication factor table \(Equiv Wt of CaCO3 = 50\):) Tj
T*
(- Ca\(HCO3\)2: Mol Wt = 162, Eq Wt = 81. Factor = 50 / 81 = 100 / 162.) Tj
T*
(CaCO3 equivalent = 16.2 * \(50 / 81\) = 10.0 mg/L \(ppm\).) Tj
T*
